window.docContents[51].resolve({"/#Functor":{"contents":"A polymorphic type is a functor if it has an overload for a function named  that transforms every element contained in it by a function.\nWhile most languages use this terminology, C#'s equivalent of  is called System.Linq.Enumerable.Select.\nFor example, mapping a function over a list constructs a new list in which each entry from the starting list has been replaced by the result of the function on that entry.\nMapping a function  over an  leaves  untouched, and replaces  with .\n\nHere are some examples of functors and how their  instances overload :\n\n*  evaluates to *  evaluates to *  evaluates to \n\nBecause  is a bit of a long name for this common operation, Lean also provides an infix operator for mapping a function, namely <$>.\nThe prior examples can be rewritten as follows:\n\n*  evaluates to *  evaluates to *  evaluates to \n\nAn instance of  for  requires specifying the  function.\n\n\n\nHere,  uses the  instance for  to map the function over the tail.\nThis instance is defined for  rather than for  because the argument type  plays no role in resolving the type class.\nA  can have a function mapped over it no matter what the type of entries is.\nIf  were a parameter to the class, then it would be possible to make versions of  that only worked for , but part of being a functor is that  works for any entry type.\n\nHere is an instance of  for :In this case,  has been applied to both  and .\n\nEven when the type contained in a functor is itself a functor, mapping a function only goes down one layer.\nThat is, when using   on a , the function being mapped should take  as its argument rather than .\n\nThe definition of the  class uses one more language feature that has not yet been discussed: default method definitions.\nNormally, a class will specify some minimal set of overloadable operations that make sense together, and then use polymorphic functions with instance implicit arguments that build on the overloaded operations to provide a larger library of features.\nFor example, the function  can concatenate any non-empty list whose entries are appendable:\n\n\n\nHowever, for some classes, there are operations that can be more efficiently implemented with knowledge of the internals of a datatype.\n\nIn these cases, a default method definition can be provided.\nA default method definition provides a default implementation of a method in terms of the other methods.\nHowever, instance implementors may choose to override this default with something more efficient.\nDefault method definitions contain := in a class definition.\n\nIn the case of , some types have a more efficient way of implementing  when the function being mapped ignores its argument.\nFunctions that ignore their arguments are called constant functions because they always return the same value.\nHere is the definition of , in which  has a default implementation:\n\n\n\nJust as a  instance that doesn't respect  is buggy, a  instance that moves around the data as it maps the function is also buggy.\nFor example, a buggy  instance for  might throw away its argument and always return the empty list, or it might reverse the list.\nA bad  instance for  might place  in both the  and the  fields, or swap them.\nSpecifically,  instances should follow two rules:\n\n1. Mapping the identity function should result in the original argument.2. Mapping two composed functions should have the same effect as composing their mapping.\n\nMore formally, the first rule says that  equals .\nThe second rule says that  equals .\nThe composition  can also be written .\nThese rules prevent implementations of  that move the data around or delete some of it.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Standard Classes","header":"3.5.7. Functors","id":"/#Functor"},"/#even-numbers-ex":{"contents":"Define a datatype that represents only even numbers. Define instances of , , and  that allow it to be used conveniently.\n requires a feature that is introduced in the next section.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Positive Numbers\u0009Exercises","header":"3.1.6.2. Even Numbers","id":"/#even-numbers-ex"},"/#executing-cartesian-product":{"contents":"Taking the Cartesian product of two tables is done by appending each row from the first table to each row from the second.\nFirst off, due to the structure of , adding a single column to a row requires pattern matching on its schema in order to determine whether the result will be a bare value or a tuple.\nBecause this is a common operation, factoring the pattern matching out into a helper is convenient:\n\n\n\nAppending two rows is recursive on the structure of both the first schema and the first row, because the structure of the row proceeds in lock-step with the structure of the schema.\nWhen the first row is empty, appending returns the second row.\nWhen the first row is a singleton, the value is added to the second row.\nWhen the first row contains multiple columns, the first column's value is added to the result of recursion on the remainder of the row.\n\n\n\n, found in the standard library, applies a function that itself returns a list to every entry in an input list, returning the result of appending the resulting lists in order:\n\n\n\nThe type signature suggests that  could be used to implement a  instance.\nIndeed, together with ,  does implement a monad.\nHowever, it's not a very useful  instance.\nThe  monad is basically a version of  that explores every possible path through the search space in advance, before users have the chance to request some number of values.\nBecause of this performance trap, it's usually not a good idea to define a  instance for .\nHere, however, the query language has no operator for restricting the number of results to be returned, so combining all possibilities is exactly what is desired:\n\n\n\nJust as with , a loop with mutation in the identity monad can be used as an alternative implementation technique:\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming with Dependent Types\u0009Worked Example: Typed Queries\u0009Executing Queries","header":"7.3.5.1. Cartesian Product","id":"/#executing-cartesian-product"},"/#leading-dot-notation":{"contents":"The constructors of an inductive type are in a namespace.\nThis allows multiple related inductive types to use the same constructor names, but it can lead to programs becoming verbose.\nIn contexts where the inductive type in question is known, the namespace can be omitted by preceding the constructor's name with a dot, and Lean uses the expected type to resolve the constructor names.\nFor example, a function that mirrors a binary tree can be written:\n\n\n\nOmitting the namespaces makes it significantly shorter, at the cost of making the program harder to read in contexts like code review tools that don't include the Lean compiler:\n\n\n\nUsing the expected type of an expression to disambiguate a namespace is also applicable to names other than constructors.\nIf  is defined as an alternative way of creating s, then it can also be used with dot notation:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monads\u0009Additional Conveniences","header":"4.6.2. Leading Dot Notation","id":"/#leading-dot-notation"}});